# wow-chat-neuron — Vision

*A branch of wow-chat-2026. Where that project built a world, this one builds
a hand that reaches into it.*

## What This Is

Everland Ghostsong (wow-chat-2026) is a world that runs. It has a server, an
emptied-out map, companions that wander it, timers that fill it with danger and
treasure. It is a game, and it works.

wow-chat-neuron is not a second game. It is the thing you talk to about the
first one.

You type a sentence. Something in the world changes.

    "put everyone in my guild on the Ratchet dock"
    "give the three of them the same axe and mail set"
    "make a party out of me, Grast, and Wenna, and hold the ridge while I'm gone"
    "spin up four more bots around level 14, rogues and hunters"
    "generate a black obelisk I can walk on, floating just off the ground,
     and several more I can jump between"

None of those are commands in any command language. They are requests. The
work of turning a request into a change is the entire project.

## The Name

A neuron takes a signal in and fires a signal out. It does not decide what the
signal means; it decides whether the conditions to fire have been met, and then
it fires, and the meaning is somewhere else, in the shape of the whole net.

That is the correct amount of ambition for this layer. It is not the game. It
is not an author. It is the place where a sentence becomes a state change.

## The Governing Constraint

The model never writes code. It never writes SQL. It never runs a shell
command. It never touches a file path of its own choosing.

It picks from a closed set of named operations with typed parameters, and the
operations do the work.

This position is inherited, not invented. It was written down a year earlier in
wow-chat-2026's `notes/wow-chat-secretary-2`, and it is still right:

> an LLM should have levers to pull, not timestamps to clobber and crush. if it
> could place files anywhere, it could be hacked. It's just a security concern,
> and it violates the bot's autonomy. [...] they can just pull pre-defined
> levers.
>
> that sort of thing makes them... not a real target. Because they can't do
> harm, so why bother trying to break them?

Read that as a security argument and it is a good one. Read it as a design
argument and it is a better one. A closed set of named operations with typed
parameters is a **vocabulary**. A vocabulary with a grammar is a **language**.
Building a small language inside the constraints of a system you do not own is
the whole craft. The safety property falls out of the design property for free;
we did not have to trade anything for it.

So the deliverable is not "an AI that plays with the database." The deliverable
is **a language for describing changes to a running world**, plus an interpreter
for it, plus — last and least important — a model that happens to be fluent.

## The Three Hands

A change reaches the world through one of three channels. Every operation
declares which hand it needs, and that declaration is what makes the operation
honest about when it can and cannot run.

### The cold hand — SQL

Direct writes to the character, world, and playerbot databases through the
project-local MySQL socket.

- Works while the server is **down**, which is the only time some facts can be
  changed at all — a running worldserver holds character state in memory and
  will happily overwrite the row you just edited when the player logs out.
- Can express things no command exists for.
- Nothing is visible until the server reads the row.

### The live hand — GM commands over SOAP

The worldserver's built-in SOAP console, bound to loopback, authenticated as a
real game account with GM rank. Every dot-command a GM could type into the
console is reachable over HTTP from a script.

- Works only while the server is **up**.
- Changes are instant and visible to everyone standing there.
- Limited to the vocabulary AzerothCore already ships, which is large.

wow-chat-2026 already turned this on and wrote down why, in
`config/patches/C021-soap-loopback-console.sh`. neuron consumes that decision
rather than re-litigating it.

### The resident hand — ALE Lua inside the worldserver

Some things are not a change; they are a *standing behavior*. A garrison that
holds a ridge, fights, dies, respawns, and walks back to the same ridge is not
one edit — it is a rule that keeps applying. Rules that keep applying have to
live inside the process that runs the world.

- Works only while the server is up, and runs on the server's tick budget.
- Hot-reloadable, because ALE reloads Lua.
- This is the only hand that can react to something happening.

## What Gets Built, In Order of Foundation

Phases are grouped by **effect** — what becomes true once the phase lands —
rather than by the order anyone happened to build them. Lower numbers are more
foundational; things above them assume things below them work.

| Phase | Once it lands...                                          |
|-------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
| 1     | The world is addressable                                  |
| 2     | Anyone can be anywhere                                    |
| 3     | Anyone can be wearing anything                            |
| 4     | Nobody is alone, and a group outlives its members         |
| 5     | A character can change without being replaced             |
| 6     | The world gains architecture the map files never had      |
| 7     | The operations become a vocabulary a machine can speak    |
| 8     | The vocabulary can be spoken by asking                    |
| 9     | Asking works from inside the game                         |
| 10    | Changes to the world become story                         |
| 11    | What was made here can be made again elsewhere            |

Phases 1 through 6 are hands and levers, and they are worth having on their own
— a person typing `neuron teleport roster.guild ratchet-dock` gets value from
phase 2 with no model involved anywhere. Phase 7 is where those levers become
describable. Phase 8 is where a sentence starts working. Phases 9 and 10 are
where it stops feeling like tooling.

The ordering has a deliberate consequence: **if the model half is never built,
the project is still useful.** That is the test of whether the levers were
designed properly.

## Style Preference Evolution

Bots are not generated once and kept. They are *renewed*.

A bot has a level, a class, a set of equipment, and — the part that does not
exist yet anywhere — a **style**: the accumulated record of what this character
has tended toward. Not a personality paragraph. A vector of leanings, built out
of what the character has actually been given and actually done.

Renewal means taking an existing character and moving it: up a few levels, or
down, re-outfitted, re-specced, with its style nudged rather than rerolled. The
same character, later. Not a replacement wearing the same name.

The reason to care: a party you have played with for a month should be
*recognizable*, and should also not be the same as it was a month ago. Those
two requirements are in tension and the tension is the feature.

## The Garrison

When a party contains a character whose player has logged out, the party does
not dissolve and the remaining bots do not go home.

They hold the position.

> the bots will remain online and guarding that position, even though the
> player NPC is missing. If an enemy approaches they will fight to the last,
> and when they come back to respawn they will defend the same position. Until
> the player from the group re-logs back in, or the server goes down.

No stand-in is spawned for the absent player. There is no mirror image, no
puppet, no sleeping body. There is a gap in the party where a person was, and
a watch that keeps standing in a place because that person said to stand there.

The absence is the point. You come back and they are still there, and some of
them died while you were gone.

## Construction

The world's geometry comes from map files that were compiled in 2008 and cannot
be edited. But gameobjects can be placed anywhere, at any height, at any scale,
and in 3.3.5a the **client** computes collision against a gameobject's model
locally. A platform spawned in mid-air is standable the instant it appears,
without the server's vmaps knowing anything about it.

That single fact is what makes "a black obelisk I can walk on, floating next to
the ground" a cheap request instead of an impossible one. It also sets the
limit: server-side pathfinding does not know the platform is there, so NPCs
will not walk onto it. Players can. Bots, mostly, cannot.

Construction operations therefore work in **arrangements**, not single objects:
a line of platforms with gaps small enough to jump, a ring, a stair, a scatter.
The arrangement is computed; the placement is a loop; the request that produced
it was one sentence.

## Narration

The last phase, and the one that justifies the rest.

Once the world can be rearranged by asking, a GM character can narrate the
rearrangement as it happens — not describing a database write, but describing
the thing the database write means:

> ... and then a horde of skeletons appeared, and we fought them for their gold
> teeth. If we take them back to camp then there's a lady who can turn each one
> into an animated skeleton by using earth magic to build out copies of bones,
> using the elemental properties of the gold token to

The sentence is unfinished. That is how the fragment was given and that is how
it is kept. Narration is not a summary written after the fact; it is a thing
said while the thing is still happening, which is why it can trail off.

The mechanical reading: a narration operation takes a world change and a voice,
and emits text into a channel as a named character. The evocative reading is the
one above, and it is the reason the mechanical one is worth building.

## Non-Goals

- **Not a second server.** neuron attaches to a deployment. It does not build,
  patch, or install AzerothCore. wow-chat-2026 does that and does it well.
- **Not arbitrary execution.** No shell, no eval, no generated SQL, no scripts
  written at request time. If an operation does not exist, the answer is that
  the operation does not exist.
- **Not a game design layer.** neuron changes the state of a world. What the
  world *is* remains wow-chat-2026's question.
- **Not a product.** As ever, the interest is in software design.

## The Promise

The levers should be good enough that pulling them by hand is satisfying.

Then the asking is a gift, not a crutch.
